In SAS programming, `nodup` is an option used with the `proc sort` procedure to eliminate duplicate observations from a dataset. 

When you use the `nodup` option with `proc sort`, SAS sorts the data and removes any observations that have the same value for the variables that you specify. The first occurrence of each unique combination of variable values is retained, and any subsequent occurrences are removed.

Here is an example of how to use `nodup` with `proc sort`:

```
proc sort data=mydata nodupkey;
    by var1 var2 var3;
run;
```

In this example, `mydata` is the name of the dataset that you want to sort and eliminate duplicates from. `nodupkey` is the option that tells SAS to remove observations with duplicate values for the variables specified in the `by` statement. `var1`, `var2`, and `var3` are the variables that you want to sort by and use to eliminate duplicates.

When `proc sort` is finished, the resulting dataset will have no duplicate observations based on the values of the variables specified in the `by` statement.

